The Four-Year Warranty

The four of them had made a pact when they were twenty-two: no matter how old they got, they would never become “the kind of people who complain about chairs.”

At thirty-five, they were breaking that pact aggressively.

They sat in mismatched lawn chairs in Marcus’s backyard, each one making a different concerning noise when someone shifted their weight.

“I don’t trust this chair,” Lena said, leaning slightly left. “It sounds judgmental.”

“You’re sitting in it wrong,” Marcus replied. “It’s from a reputable brand. I bought it when I moved in.”

“When you moved in when?” asked Theo.

Marcus frowned. “Don’t do that.”

“Seven years ago,” Nina said, checking her phone. “I just looked at the photos. That chair has seen things.”

They all adjusted carefully, like astronauts testing gravity.

They had been friends since college, bonded by late nights, cheap food, and the shared belief that adulthood would eventually explain itself. It hadn’t. Instead, it kept adding subscriptions and joint pain.

Theo stretched and immediately regretted it.

“Why does stretching hurt now?” he asked. “Isn’t this supposed to prevent pain?”

“No,” Lena said. “Stretching reveals pain.”

Nina nodded. “I stretched this morning and remembered an injury from 2011.”

They sat quietly for a moment, listening to the hum of the city and Marcus’s neighbor aggressively power-washing something that clearly did not need it.

The conversation drifted, as it always did, to memories.

“Remember when we used to stay out until 2 a.m.?” Marcus said.

“Remember when 2 a.m. was a time and not a threat?” Lena replied.

Theo laughed, then winced. “I need a nap after laughing now.”

Nina raised her drink. “To aging. May it continue slowly and with decent lighting.”

They clinked cans gently. No one trusted glass anymore.

Later, they decided—against their better judgment—to play a game of backyard frisbee.

“This will be fine,” Marcus said, already out of breath.

Theo threw the frisbee too hard. It soared beautifully, majestically, and landed on the roof.

They all stared up at it.

“Well,” Nina said, “that’s where it lives now.”

Marcus sighed. “I’m not climbing up there.”

“None of us should,” Lena said. “We have jobs.”

They agreed this was the responsible choice and went back to sitting down, congratulating themselves on the wisdom of restraint.

As the sun set, the jokes softened. The laughter slowed but deepened, settling into something comfortable.

They talked about work, families, plans they were still pretending to make. Not everything was easy. Not everything was funny.

But being together still was.

Marcus looked around at them—at Theo rubbing his knee, Lena carefully standing up in stages, Nina setting a phone reminder to stretch tomorrow.

“Hey,” he said. “We’re doing okay, right?”

Nina smiled. “Yeah. We’re just the same people with worse warranties.”

They laughed again, carefully this time.

And when they finally left—earlier than planned, naturally—they did so knowing that getting older hadn’t broken their friendship.

It had just added sound effects.